{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>You are standing.</b> One must know the intent of this statement. Further, why was it necessary to specify <b>your heads</b> etc. <b>your little ones</b> etc., and it would not have sufficed to say in general terms ״all of you״? Further, one must know the reason for this covenant — was it not said nearby: ״אֵלֶּה דִבְרֵי הַבְּרִית״ (Deuteronomy 28:69)? And if He wished to include as well what is stated in this passage within the covenant, He should have prefaced the matter and said over all of it ״אֵלֶּה דִבְרֵי הַבְּרִית״." "<b>And it appears</b> that the intent of Moses in this covenant is to bring them into mutual surety (arvut), one for the other, so that each person would exert himself on behalf of his fellow, lest he transgress the word of HaShem, and they would be held accountable one for the other. And the faithful witness to this is what the verse concludes: ״הַנִּסְתָּרֹת לַה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ וְהַנִּגְלֹת לָנוּ״ etc. (Deuteronomy 29:28) — see that the verse speaks of surety. And this is not the covenant that He spoke of at the end of parashat Tavo, for that covenant is upon themselves, whereas this one obligates each person for his Hebrew brother according to the capacity that is in the hand of each one." "<b>And now</b> we have come to understand the statement <b>you are standing</b>, that the intent is after the manner of its saying (Ruth 2:5) ״הַנִּצָּב עַל הַקּוֹצְרִים״ — a term of appointment. And His saying <b>all of you</b> means: all of them bear this yoke, each one according to what he is, after the manner of their saying, of blessed memory, in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 54a): everyone who is able to protest against the entire world is held accountable for all etc., and everyone who is able to protest against the people of his city is held accountable for the people of his city, and everyone who is able to protest against the members of his household is held accountable for etc. And this is what the verse specified here and said <b>your heads</b> etc. — that each one is obligated according to what is in his hand. <b>Your heads</b> — these are the greatest among all, who are able to protest against all of Israel; these are obligated for everything. <b>Your tribes</b> — each tribe is obligated for its tribe. <b>Your elders and your officers</b> — each elder is obligated for his clan. <b>Every man of Israel</b> — these are the multitudes; each one is obligated for the members of his household. Therefore the verse broke off here and arranged <b>your little ones</b> etc. separately, for others are held accountable for them — they being those enumerated in the preceding verse, each one according to what he is — but they themselves are not held accountable for others, since the little ones are not of understanding, and the women likewise; and converts as well have no authority to rule over Israel, as they expounded, of blessed memory (Yevamot 45a), on the verse (Deuteronomy 17:15) ״שׂוֹם תָּשִׂים עָלֶיךָ״ etc. — all appointments that you set over yourself shall be only from among your brothers and not converts; but Israel are obligated for them, as they said, of blessed memory (Niddah 13a): converts are as burdensome to Israel as a sore, and they explained it to mean that they do not teach them everything they need — thus you have learned that they are held accountable for them." "<b>Further,</b> He alluded in His saying <b>you are standing</b> etc. before HaShem, for everything that is distant from holiness — its stature is lowered and its head is bent low (Genesis Rabbah 12:6); therefore He said that in order to be before HaShem they stood and lifted up their heads."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:01.013860015Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}